  "_comment": "Every kind of notice, the lengths a server or room can be muted for, and the sounds a notice can play (docs/design/notifications.md). Read by the desktop app (src/notifications/data.rs) and the website (web/shared/notify-rules.js). A kind's words are keys in data/i18n/en.json. `built` says whether anything raises the kind yet; `server_flag` is the synced switch (notification_prefs) that turns it on or off; `scope` is person (a conversation: never muted by a server or room mute; muting that conversation stops it), room (a server's room: server and room mutes apply) or game. A room kind's `level` is the least a room must be set to for it to notify (nothing < mentions < all; a room is at mentions unless the person sets it otherwise), so `room_line`, an ordinary line, notifies only in a room set to every line. `quiet_exception` names the synced quiet-hours exception that lets the kind through quiet hours: friends (from a friend: you follow each other) or mentions; none when absent. A mute of 0 seconds lasts until the person unmutes. `levels` are the choices a room's right-click offers, in order, each with its words. Per-person controls (2026-11-03, docs/design/per-person-controls.md): a kind's `person_level` is the least a SENDER's own level (Notifications from them, on the profile card) must be for the kind to notify (absent: all), so someone set to mentions (Only calls, mentions and replies) still makes the kinds marked mentions notify and nothing else, and someone set to nothing makes none; `person_levels` are the card's choices, in order. A sound's id is its entry in data/sounds.toml; `path` is used when that entry is missing. Rung 3 (2026-11-01): a kind's `sound` is its default sound (an id in `sounds`; the person picks another, or none, per kind on this device); `burst` false means the five-second burst guard does not apply (a ring is one event per call); scope `server` is a server's own record (its mute applies; no room level and no conversation mute: a task). A kind's `variants` name another title for a candidate's variant (a task newly given to you: `assigned`). `game_events` are what the simulation raises (systems::push_game_event), each with its default `on`, `every_secs` (the fold: at most one notice of it per interval, notifications::game), the page a click opens (`opens`, a page id; empty: the world) and its words: `title`, and `body` read with a count (`.one` and `.other`, with {{n}} and {{where}}).",
